\caption(Candidate scores, RCL membership, choices and every flip test come from one producing run. Only the constructed objective is a separate named diagnostic; the producing kernel evaluates its objective after local search.)
\alttext(Two weighted triangles show actual constructed and final side labels. Flipping vertex zero replaces a crossing edge of weight one by one of weight two, increasing the cut from four to five.)

> Derive construction and the flip gain

For a symmetric loopless weighted graph, a partition s∈{0,1}ⁿ has objective

$$C(s)=\sum_{u<v} w_{uv}\,[s_u\ne s_v].$$
\alttext(Sum each undirected edge weight once when its endpoints have different side labels; parallel records contribute separately.)

Signed, zero and parallel weights are supported. Input rows must contain
reciprocal entries with matching weights and multiplicities, while their
original order is retained for floating-point sums. Self-loops are rejected:
treating a loop as an incident flip gain would be wrong, and the old code could
flip such a vertex forever.

For each unassigned vertex v, construction computes g₀(v) and g₁(v) from edges
to already assigned neighbors on the opposite side. Its candidate score is
g(v)=max(g₀,g₁), with side zero winning exact side-score ties. Let gmax and gmin
be the extreme candidate scores. The implemented restricted candidate list is

$$\theta=g_{\max}-\alpha(g_{\max}-g_{\min}),\qquad
\mathrm{RCL}=\{v:g(v)\ge\theta-10^{-12}\}.$$
\alttext(The cutoff moves from the best gain toward the worst as alpha increases, with an additional absolute tolerance of ten to the minus twelve in the membership test.)

A single xorshift word modulo the RCL length chooses a candidate. Modulo
reduction is generally biased. Alpha zero randomizes equal or nearly equal best
candidates; it is not a deterministic single choice. Alpha one ideally admits
all candidates, but the actual threshold is rounded. The first placement has no
assigned neighbors, so every candidate initially ties at zero regardless of
alpha. After each placement, remaining scores are recomputed, making
construction adaptive.

For a complete partition, flipping v reverses each incident crossing indicator.
The exact gain is

$$\Delta_v=\sum_{u\sim v}w_{vu}
\begin{cases}+1,&s_u=s_v,\\-1,&s_u\ne s_v.\end{cases}$$
\alttext(An incident edge enters the cut when its endpoints were on the same side, and leaves the cut when they were on opposite sides.)

The native local phase scans vertices in ascending order and immediately accepts
a computed gain greater than 10⁻¹². Later tests in the same pass see earlier
flips. It repeats full scans until one accepts no flip. The terminal partition
therefore passes all computed single-flip tolerance tests; it is not necessarily
an exact-real local optimum, let alone a global one.

In exact arithmetic, every accepted positive move increases C on a finite
partition space, so cycling is impossible. Rounded gains can disagree with a
rounded objective difference, so that proof alone does not establish
floating-point termination. A configurable per-restart pass cap includes the
terminal no-flip scan; the default and legacy call use one million passes.
Exhaustion throws rather than returning a falsely finished local search.

> A local trap is not a global certificate

On an unweighted four-cycle, partition (0,0,1,1) cuts two edges. Every vertex
has one crossing and one noncrossing incident edge, so every flip gain is
exactly zero. Single-flip strict improvement stops there. Alternating sides cut
all four edges. This is a diagnostic example of the neighborhood's limitation,
not a claim that the preceding triangle run visited that partition.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/grasp_local_trap.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    const dm::algorithms::GraspAdjacency cycle{{{1, 1}, {3, 1}},
                                               {{0, 1}, {2, 1}},
                                               {{1, 1}, {3, 1}},
                                               {{2, 1}, {0, 1}}};
    const auto trap = dm::algorithms::grasp_cut_check(cycle, {0, 0, 1, 1});
    const auto exact = dm::algorithms::grasp_exact_max_cut(cycle);
    std::print("| vertex | supplied-cut flip gain |\n| ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t v = 0; v < trap.flip_gains.size(); ++v)
        std::print("| {} | {} |\n", v, trap.flip_gains[v]);
    std::print("\nSupplied cut {}; computed tolerance-local {}; exhaustive best {}; complement classes " "examined {}.\n",
               trap.value, trap.tolerance_local, exact.result.value,
               exact.partitions);
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.bars({1, 2}, {trap.value, exact.result.value}, "cut value", {37, 99, 235}).title("A single-flip local trap loses half the four-cycle cut").x_label("1 supplied local trap; 2 exhaustive optimum").y_label("crossing edge weight").y_limits(0, 5).save_svg("build/grasp_local_trap.svg");
    if (trap.value != 2 || !trap.tolerance_local || exact.result.value != 4)
        throw std::runtime_error("local trap");
}
