\image(figures/grasp_work.svg)
// result_end
\caption(All rows use ten restarts on the tiny weighted triangle. Alpha changes candidate eligibility and therefore local work, while the number of consumed construction RNG words remains exactly thirty.)
\alttext(Three bars compare actual full local scans for alpha zero, 0.3 and one. Terminal no-flip scans are included; the table also verifies exact candidate, RNG and capture formulas.)

A seed initializes one continuing xorshift stream, not independent fresh seeds
per restart. Repeating a larger restart budget with the same input, seed and
options has the same earlier prefix; this property is checked separately. Modulo
bias, ties and the absolute slacks make general success-probability claims
inappropriate. Independent restarts can be a modeling assumption in analysis,
but are not a proved statistical property of this finite deterministic
generator.

Basic validation rejects negative or mismatched n, invalid endpoints, loops,
nonfinite weights, nonpositive restart counts and alpha outside finite [0,1],
before input caps. Reciprocal weights and multiplicities are checked after
complete input caps and before search; duplicates retain their original order.
Default complete caps are one million vertices and directed entries, each zero
disabling it. The legacy call has no input/capture policy cap but retains the
per-restart pass guard. New options can disable that guard with zero, which
removes its floating termination protection. Nonfinite accumulated scores,
cutoff ranges, deltas or objectives throw. Failures return no partial
result/history, and floating flags already raised are not rolled back.

The bounded exhaustive helper is a verification tool, with a hard 24-vertex
limit and a default one-million complement-class cap checked before enumeration.
Exact means all partitions are considered, not exact arithmetic for arbitrary
floating weights. The integer fixtures here avoid that ambiguity. Empty graphs
return value zero; signed and parallel finite weights are otherwise supported
under the validated graph contract.

> What this run establishes

The tiny trace shows adaptive construction followed by an actual immediate
improving flip. The four-cycle shows why a single-flip stopping test does not
certify a global optimum. The original graph reaches its exhaustive reference in
this run, and prefix retention is monotone by construction. None of that
establishes reliable optimality for unseen graphs, a universal benefit from a
particular alpha, or a runtime guarantee for local search. Controlled synthetic
instances fit these questions without forcing an unrelated dataset.

The primary reference is T. A. Feo and M. G. C. Resende, [Greedy Randomized
Adaptive Search Procedures](https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01096763) (1995), with an
[author-hosted copy](https://mauricio.resende.info/doc/gtut.pdf). Its generic
construction/local-search/incumbent framework motivates this variant. The
specific weighted scoring, cyclic immediate flips, decision slacks, RNG stream
and resource guards are this library's implementation policy.

Verification compares legacy valid outputs with a frozen pre-conversion
implementation, reconstructs actual RCL scores, RNG states, immediate flips,
prefix incumbents and selected work, and uses independent exhaustive masks for
small signed graphs. Original-graph enumeration, malformed input, loop
termination, quota, pass-cap, overflow and ownership checks accompany sanitizer
runs. Complete report programs, figures, API examples and HTML/Markdown exports
are checked before the legacy files are retired.
